 A classic programming interview question that involves using array methods 
 (filter, map, reduce), and JavaScript objects.

 assume Given an array of employees, use JavaScript array methods to:

 "filter() is used to select elements based on a condition and returns a new array. 
 map() is used to transform each element and returns a new array. 
 reduce() is used to accumulate the elements of an array into a single result, such as a sum or count."

Find employees whose salary is greater than 50,000.
Get only their names.
Calculate their total salary.

const employees = [
    { name: "John", salary: 45000 },
    { name: "Sara", salary: 60000 },
    { name: "Mike", salary: 70000 },
    { name: "Anna", salary: 40000 }
];
const filteredEmployees = employees.filter(employee => employee.salary > 50000);
console.log(filteredEmployees);
//filter() → gives you a subset of the array.
const names = filteredEmployees.map(employee => employee.name);
console.log(names);
//map() → transforms every element and returns a new array.
const totalSalary = filteredEmployees.reduce(
    (total, employee) => total + employee.salary,
    0
);
console.log(totalSalary);
//reduce() → reduces an array to one final value.